<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The MSI, for WiX 6 (`wix build`; see msi.ps1, which is the only thing meant to
  invoke this).

  Why an MSI: it installs to Program Files with a Start Menu entry, it appears in
  Programs and Features so a machine can be asked what version it has, it upgrades
  in place, and it can be pushed by Group Policy or Intune with nobody at the
  keyboard.

  **On Windows it is now the only artefact** (decided 2026-09-08). A portable zip
  shipped beside it until then, for the operator who cannot install software on
  their own machine. The elevated FIDO2 helper below ended that: a service can only
  be installed by an installer, so the zip would be permanently unable to perform a
  third of the standard procedure plus the factory reset. features/packaging-and-
  release.md justifies two artefacts per platform on the grounds that they have
  *identical capabilities*, and warns in the same bullet against shipping two that
  do not — so withdrawing it is that rule applied. macOS and Linux keep both.

  Deliberate choices, each of which has a failure mode behind it:

  * `Scope="perMachine"`. A register of security tokens is installed on a
    workstation, not into one person's profile, and a per-user install in
    AppData is invisible to the next operator who sits down at it.
  * `MajorUpgrade` with a downgrade message. Without it, installing 0.16 over 0.15
    leaves two entries in Programs and Features and two copies on disk, and which
    one the shortcut points at is a coin toss.
  * The version comes in as a preprocessor variable from Cargo.toml
    (single source of truth), never typed here.
  * `UpgradeCode` is a literal, generated once, and must never change: it is the
    identity by which every future version recognises this product. A new one
    means the next release does not upgrade this one, it installs beside it.
  * `WixUI_InstallDir` — a wizard with a licence pane and a choice of folder, which
    is what somebody double-clicking an installer expects. It is also what makes
    the silent install (`msiexec /qn INSTALLFOLDER=...`) meaningful, which is how
    verify-msi.ps1 checks the thing.
  * No file association, no PATH entry, no registry beyond what the installer
    needs. The tool reads and writes one SQLite file that the operator chooses;
    there is nothing here to configure.
  * **One service** (features/windows-elevated-helper.md).
    This bullet used to say "no service", and that was right until Windows made it
    impossible: `hidclass` refuses a process that is not elevated any handle to a
    security key's FIDO2 interface, so an ordinary operator running this
    application cannot set a FIDO2 PIN, create the initial credential or perform
    the FIDO2 third of a factory reset. There is no per-device permission to grant
    the way Linux has, and `ykman fido` is refused for the same reason, so the only
    lever is elevation. Demanding it of every operator, at every key, is the wrong
    trade; spending it **once, here, by the account that is already installing
    software** is the right one. That is also why the portable zip was withdrawn on
    Windows (decided 2026-09-08): a service can only be installed by an installer,
    so a zip could never do a third of the procedure.

  There is no `Manufacturer` naming an institution, and there cannot be: the
  application carries no organisation's name — that is the operator's setting
  (roadmap decision, 2026-08-11) — and packaging is exactly the place a name comes
  back with nothing in the test suite to notice. So the manufacturer matches the
  .deb's maintainer field, and a unit that wants its own sets YKDM_MANUFACTURER at
  build time.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:ui="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/ui">

  <Package
      Name="YubiKey Distribution Manager"
      Manufacturer="$(Manufacturer)"
      Version="$(Version)"
      UpgradeCode="132275b3-f866-4be4-bc2f-87090ead2fb7"
      Scope="perMachine"
      Compressed="yes">

    <MajorUpgrade
        AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes"
        DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of [ProductName] is already installed on this machine. Uninstall it first if you mean to go back to an older one." />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <!-- Programs and Features: the icon, and no support URL, because a link that
         goes stale is worse than none. The diagnose flag is how a build identifies
         itself, and the Read Me installed beside the executable says so. -->
    <!-- The Id doubles as the Icon table's name, and Windows Installer decides how
         to read the icon from its extension — so an .ico source needs an Id ending
         in .ico, or the shell renders nothing and says nothing. Everything that
         wants this icon names that Id: the property below and the Start Menu
         shortcut, which is one row of the Icon table and not one each. -->
    <Icon Id="AppIcon.ico" SourceFile="$(IconFile)" />
    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="AppIcon.ico" />
    <!-- Nothing here needs a reboot, and an installer that asks for one when it
         does not is an installer people stop trusting. -->
    <Property Id="REBOOT" Value="ReallySuppress" />

    <ui:WixUI Id="WixUI_InstallDir" InstallDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER" />
    <WixVariable Id="WixUILicenseRtf" Value="$(LicenseRtf)" />

    <!-- ProgramFiles6432Folder resolves to the 64-bit Program Files for the x64
         build and the 32-bit one for an x86 build, so the architecture is decided
         by `wix build -arch` and stated in exactly one place. -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles6432Folder">
      <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="YubiKey Distribution Manager" />
    </StandardDirectory>
    <!-- Declared because the shortcut below refers to it. The Start Menu entry sits
         directly in Programs rather than in a folder of its own: one application
         does not need a folder, and a folder needs a component whose only purpose
         is to remove it again on uninstall. -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramMenuFolder" />

    <Feature Id="Main" Title="YubiKey Distribution Manager" Level="1">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="ApplicationFiles" />
    </Feature>
  </Package>

  <Fragment>
    <ComponentGroup Id="ApplicationFiles" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Component>
        <!-- The shortcut is a child of the File, so it targets that file and the
             component's key path is the executable. The alternative — a shortcut in
             its own component — needs a registry value invented purely to be a key
             path, which is a thing to explain forever. -->
        <File Id="MainExecutable" Source="$(ExeFile)" KeyPath="yes">
          <Shortcut
              Id="StartMenuShortcut"
              Directory="ProgramMenuFolder"
              Name="YubiKey Distribution Manager"
              Description="Register of security tokens: who holds which key, and what was applied to it"
              Icon="AppIcon.ico"
              WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER"
              Advertise="no" />
        </File>

        <!-- The elevated FIDO2 helper (features/windows-elevated-helper.md).
             The same executable as the GUI, started with a flag: two files would
             mean the running service could be a different build from the
             application that just replaced it, and would need a second
             Authenticode signature.

             Four attributes carry decisions rather than defaults:

             * Account="LocalSystem" is what certainly holds the privilege. It is
               more privilege than the job needs, and a virtual service account
               (NT SERVICE\YkDistManagerFido) is the intended end state — whether
               one can open the FIDO interface cannot be determined without a
               Windows host and a key, so it is phase 6 rather than a guess here.
             * Vital="no" so a service that will not install or start does not fail
               the whole installation. The application is useful without it — the
               register, PIV and OTP all work — and it says on the pre-flight
               screen which steps it cannot perform. An installer that refuses to
               put the tool on the machine at all would be a worse answer to the
               same fault.
             * Start="install" with Stop="both": started when installed, and
               stopped on the way *out* of both an uninstall and the removal half
               of a MajorUpgrade. That pairing is what restarts it on upgrade,
               which is the one case the protocol's version handshake exists to
               catch — a service left running from the previous build.
             * Remove="uninstall". A LocalSystem service surviving an uninstall is
               the worst outcome available here, and verify-msi.ps1 asserts it is
               gone. -->
        <ServiceInstall
            Id="Fido2HelperService"
            Name="YkDistManagerFido"
            DisplayName="YubiKey Distribution Manager FIDO2 helper"
            Description="Performs FIDO2 operations on an attached YubiKey for the YubiKey Distribution Manager, which Windows does not permit an unelevated application to do."
            Type="ownProcess"
            Start="auto"
            Account="LocalSystem"
            ErrorControl="normal"
            Arguments="--windows-service"
            Vital="no" />
        <ServiceControl
            Id="Fido2HelperControl"
            Name="YkDistManagerFido"
            Start="install"
            Stop="both"
            Remove="uninstall"
            Wait="yes" />
      </Component>

      <!-- The licence and the changelog travel with the build, as they do in the
           macOS and Linux packages, and README.install is the Windows copy of
           the notes that have to be in front of somebody at the machine rather
           than only in docs/operations.md. -->
      <Component>
        <File Source="$(LicenseFile)" Name="LICENSE.txt" />
      </Component>
      <Component>
        <File Source="$(ChangelogFile)" Name="CHANGELOG.md" />
      </Component>
      <Component>
        <File Source="$(ReadmeFile)" Name="README.install.txt" />
      </Component>
      <Component>
        <File Source="$(IconFile)" Name="icon.ico" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
  </Fragment>
</Wix>
